(** The documentation of the natives, which have no source in Cesia: cesia doc reads it, and cesia test runs its examples.
    Each one has a block whose first line is its signature. Nothing here is code but the uses. *)
use std/sys
use std/json
use std/regex
use std/random
use std/columns
use std/debug
use std/net
use std/c
use std/lapack
use std/cesia
use std/bug
use std/dict
use std/fiber
use std/list
use std/math
use std/matrix
use std/result
use std/text
use std/value

(** io
    Writing out: Print shows values on the standard output. *)

(** bug
    Bugs: Panic and Assert stop the fiber, with a message. A bug is not an error value: an Err is returned, a panic is not. *)

(** text
    Texts: split, join, search, slice and change them, by characters or by bytes. Positions count from 1. *)

(** matrix
    Matrices of floats: build them, reshape them, transpose and multiply them. *)

(** sys
    Files, folders, processes, the environment and the clock: what a program touches outside itself. Each function says the
    permission it needs: --allow read, write, exec or env, or a path or a name after =. *)

(** json
    JSON text to values and back: objects become dictionaries, arrays lists, and null missing. *)

(** regex
    Regular expressions that stay linear whatever the pattern and the text: no backtracking, so no pattern can hang a
    program. Positions count characters, from 1. *)

(** columns
    The work on whole columns behind std/frame and std/sql, in OCaml: CSV, groups, joins and rows that share their keys. A
    column of integers or floats is stored without a box. *)

(** debug
    The breakpoints and the input of a debugger given to cesia run --debugger: needs the debug permission. *)

(** net
    TCP: listen, accept, connect, read and write, each waiting in its fiber only. Needs --allow net, or net=:PORT. std/http
    builds on it. *)

(** c
    C libraries, called from Cesia: open a library, bind its functions by their types, and pass memory, structures and
    callbacks. Calling C is total trust: it ignores every permission and limit, and a wrong signature can crash the program.
    Needs --allow ffi=NAME. *)

(** lapack
    LAPACK, when the machine has it: solve, determinant, QR and eigenvalues of matrices. std/num uses it when it is there,
    and its own code when it isn't. *)

(** cesia
    Cesia about itself: its version, its binary, its libraries, the fingerprints of cesia.lock, cesia.toml read as tables,
    the uses of a source, and the documentation as the docs site reads it. *)

(** sqlite3
    SQLite's natives, which std/sql builds on: pools of connections, statements, cursors and scripts. Loaded at the first
    call, so that a program that doesn't use it never pays for it. Needs read and write on the path of a database, and never
    ffi. *)

(** math.Pi
    The ratio of a circle to its diameter.

      math.Pi > 3.14 --> true *)

(** list.Sort(xs)
    The elements in increasing order, keeping the order of equal ones.

      list.Sort([3, 1, 2]) --> [1, 2, 3] *)

(** sys.ReadInput()
    All that the standard input holds, up to its end, in an Ok, or an Err: cat data.csv | cesia run clean.ce. The program
    waits while it reads, its other fibers too. It needs no permission: the input is the program's own. *)

(** sys.Exit(code)
    Ends the program at once, with an exit code from 0 to 255: 0 says that all went well, another number that something
    failed, for the shell or the program that ran it. What was printed is written out first. *)

(** sys.Now()
    The time in seconds since 1970, as a float.

      sys.Now() > 0.0 --> true *)

(** io.Print(...)
    Writes the texts of its arguments on one line, separated by spaces. *)

(** io.PrintError(...)
    Writes as io.Print does, on the standard error: for what went wrong, which a pipe or a file that takes the output leaves
    out, as in cesia run tool.ce > out.txt. *)

(** text.Str(...)
    The texts of its arguments, joined with nothing between.

      text.Str("a", 1, true) --> "a1true" *)

(** value.Text(x)
    The text of a value, as print shows it.

      value.Text(12) --> "12" *)

(** bug.Panic(message)
    Stops the fiber with a user error: a bug, not a value to handle. *)

(** bug.Assert(condition, message = missing)
    Does nothing if the condition is true. Otherwise it panics, with the message if there is one.

      bug.Assert(1 < 2) --> missing *)

(** sys.Args
    The command-line arguments after the script, as an array of texts. *)

(** cesia.Doc(name)
    The signature and the comment of a name given as text ("sort", "num.Solve", "date.Date.Year") or of a function, or missing if
    there is none. A method of the running program is found by its text too: "Rect.Area", "Rect:Square".

      text.Contains("One more", cesia.Doc("math.Inc")) --> true *)

(** cesia.DocNames(module = missing)
    The public names documented in a module, or the names of all the modules without an argument.

      "math.Inc" in cesia.DocNames("std/math") --> true *)

(** fiber.Spawn(f)
    Starts f() in a new fiber and gives a task to wait for. *)

(** fiber.Wait(task)
    The result of a task, once it is done: an Ok of its value, or an Err if the fiber panicked.

      fiber.Wait(fiber.Spawn(() -> 1)) --> result.Ok(1) *)

(** fiber.Channel()
    A new channel: a queue of values between fibers. *)

(** fiber.Send(value, ch)
    Puts a value at the end of a channel. It never waits, and wakes the first fiber waiting on it. *)

(** fiber.Receive(ch, timeout = missing)
    An Ok of the oldest value of a channel. With none, the fiber waits for one, at most timeout seconds when given: then an Err of kind "timeout". A timeout of 0 never waits. *)

(** value.Len(x)
    The number of elements of an array, of characters of a text, of pairs of a dictionary, or of fields of a struct; 1 for any other value.

      value.Len("héllo") --> 5 *)

(** value.Size(x, dim = missing)
    The rows and columns of an array as [rows, cols], or only the rows (1) or the columns (2). A list has one row.

      value.Size([1, 2, 3], 2) --> 3 *)

(** matrix.Reshape(xs, rows, cols)
    The same elements, filled column by column into a new shape. The count must stay the same.

      value.Size(matrix.Reshape([1, 2, 3, 4], 2, 2)) --> [2, 2] *)

(** matrix.Zeros(rows, cols = missing)
    An array of 0.0: a list of n with one argument, rows by columns with two.

      matrix.Zeros(2) --> [0.0, 0.0] *)

(** matrix.Ones(rows, cols = missing)
    An array of 1.0: a list of n with one argument, rows by columns with two.

      matrix.Ones(2) --> [1.0, 1.0] *)

(** list.Fill(value, rows, cols = missing)
    An array where every element is the value: a list of n with one size, rows by columns with two.

      list.Fill("a", 2) --> ["a", "a"] *)

(** matrix.Matmul(a, b)
    The matrix product of two arrays, as floats. The columns of a must match the rows of b.

      matrix.Matmul(matrix.Ones(1, 2), matrix.Ones(2, 1))[1] --> 2.0 *)

(** matrix.Transpose(m)
    The array with rows and columns swapped.

      value.Size(matrix.Transpose([1, 2, 3])) --> [3, 1] *)

(** list.Sum(xs)
    The sum of the elements of an array.

      list.Sum([1, 2, 3]) --> 6 *)

(** list.Mean(xs)
    The mean of the elements of an array, which is nan if it is empty.

      list.Mean([1, 2, 3]) --> 2 *)

(** list.Min(xs)
    The smallest element of an array, or with two arguments list.Min(a, b), the smaller of each pair. A missing value gives missing.

      list.Min([3, 1, 2]) --> 1 *)

(** list.Max(xs)
    The largest element of an array, or with two arguments list.Max(a, b), the larger of each pair. A missing value gives missing.

      list.Max([3, 1, 2]) --> 3 *)

(** list.Any(mask)
    Is some element of an array of booleans true?

      list.Any([false, true]) --> true *)

(** list.All(mask)
    Are all the elements of an array of booleans true?

      list.All([true, false]) --> false *)

(** math.Abs(x)
    The absolute value of a number, element by element for an array.

      math.Abs(-3) --> 3 *)

(** math.Sign(x)
    -1, 0 or 1 by the sign of a number, element by element for an array.

      math.Sign(-3) --> -1 *)

(** math.Sqrt(x)
    The square root, as a float, element by element for an array.

      math.Sqrt(9) --> 3.0 *)

(** math.Exp(x)
    e to the power x, as a float, element by element for an array.

      math.Exp(0) --> 1.0 *)

(** math.Log(x)
    The natural logarithm, as a float, element by element for an array.

      math.Log(1) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Sin(x)
    The sine of an angle in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Sin(0) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Cos(x)
    The cosine of an angle in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Cos(0) --> 1.0 *)

(** math.Tan(x)
    The tangent of an angle in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Tan(0) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Asin(x)
    The arc sine, in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Asin(0) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Acos(x)
    The arc cosine, in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Acos(1) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Atan(x)
    The arc tangent, in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Atan(0) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Atan2(y, x)
    The angle in radians of the point (x, y), from -pi to pi.

      math.Atan2(0, 1) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Floor(x)
    The largest whole number not above x. An integer stays an integer.

      math.Floor(2.7) --> 2.0 *)

(** math.Ceil(x)
    The smallest whole number not below x. An integer stays an integer.

      math.Ceil(2.1) --> 3.0 *)

(** math.Round(x)
    The nearest whole number, halves away from zero. An integer stays an integer.

      math.Round(2.5) --> 3.0 *)

(** math.Div(a, b)
    The integer quotient of a by b, rounded down. Dividing by 0 is an error.

      math.Div(-7, 2) --> -4 *)

(** math.Mod(a, b)
    The remainder of a by b, with the sign of b, so that a == math.Div(a, b) * b + math.Mod(a, b).

      math.Mod(-7, 2) --> 1 *)

(** math.Nan
    The float that is not a number.

      math.Nan == math.Nan --> false *)

(** value.Equal(a, b)
    Are two values equal in content, whatever their kind of array or struct?

      value.Equal([1, 2], [1, 2]) --> true *)

(** value.IsInt(x)
    Is this value an integer?

      value.IsInt(1) --> true *)

(** value.IsFloat(x)
    Is this value a float?

      value.IsFloat(1) --> false *)

(** value.IsNumber(x)
    Is this value an integer or a float?

      value.IsNumber(1.5) --> true *)

(** value.IsBool(x)
    Is this value true or false?

      value.IsBool(true) --> true *)

(** value.IsText(x)
    Is this value a text?

      value.IsText("a") --> true *)

(** value.IsArray(x)
    Is this value an array?

      value.IsArray([1]) --> true *)

(** value.IsDict(x)
    Is this value a dictionary, written {Name: 1} or {"name": 1}, or built by Dict or json.Parse?

      value.IsDict({A: 1}) --> true *)

(** value.IsStruct(x)
    Is this value a struct, declared by a struct? A dictionary is not.

      value.IsStruct(result.Ok(1)) --> true *)

(** dict.Fields(s)
    The fields of a struct as a dictionary, in their order: its public fields, and inside its package its small ones too.
    User.From takes it back.

      dict.Fields(result.Ok(1)) == {Value: 1} --> true *)

(** value.IsFunction(x)
    Is this value a function, native ones included?

      value.IsFunction(math.Inc) --> true *)

(** value.IsMissing(x)
    Is this value missing?

      value.IsMissing(missing) --> true *)

(** dict.Dict(pairs = missing)
    A dictionary from an array of structs with a Key and a Value (see Pairs), or an empty one.

      dict.Dict([{Key: "a", Value: 1}])["a"] --> 1 *)

(** dict.Get(key, d)
    The value of a key in a dictionary, or missing if it is absent.

      dict.Get("a", {"a": 1}) --> 1 *)

(** dict.GetOr(key, default, d)
    The value of a key in a dictionary, or the default if it is absent.

      dict.GetOr("b", 0, {"a": 1}) --> 0 *)

(** dict.Has(key, d)
    Does the dictionary have this key?

      dict.Has("a", {"a": 1}) --> true *)

(** dict.Put(key, value, d)
    A dictionary with the key set to the value. The original is unchanged.

      dict.Put("a", 2, {"a": 1})["a"] --> 2 *)

(** dict.Drop(key, d)
    A dictionary without the key. The original is unchanged.

      dict.Has("a", dict.Drop("a", {"a": 1})) --> false *)

(** dict.Keys(d)
    The keys of a dictionary, as an array, in the order of insertion: writing a key that is there keeps its place.

      dict.Keys({"b": 1, "a": 2}) --> ["b", "a"] *)

(** dict.Values(d)
    The values of a dictionary, as an array, in the order of its keys.

      dict.Values({"a": 1}) --> [1] *)

(** dict.Pairs(d)
    The entries of a dictionary, as an array of structs with a Key and a Value.

      dict.Pairs({"a": 1})[1].Value --> 1 *)

(** list.Append(x, xs)
    A list with x added at the end.

      list.Append(3, [1, 2]) --> [1, 2, 3] *)

(** list.Concat(a, b)
    The elements of a followed by the elements of b.

      list.Concat([1], [2, 3]) --> [1, 2, 3] *)

(** list.Reverse(xs)
    The elements in the opposite order.

      list.Reverse([1, 2, 3]) --> [3, 2, 1] *)

(** list.Take(n, xs)
    The first n elements, or all of them if there are fewer.

      list.Take(2, [1, 2, 3]) --> [1, 2] *)

(** list.Skip(n, xs)
    The elements after the first n.

      list.Skip(1, [1, 2, 3]) --> [2, 3] *)

(** list.IndexOf(x, xs)
    The position, from 1, of the first element equal to x, or missing.

      list.IndexOf(3, [1, 3]) --> 2 *)

(** list.Order(xs)
    The positions, from 1, that put the elements in increasing order. Stable.

      list.Order([30, 10, 20]) --> [2, 3, 1] *)

(** list.Unique(xs)
    The elements without repeats, keeping the first of each.

      list.Unique([1, 2, 1]) --> [1, 2] *)

(** list.Flatten(xs)
    The elements of xs, where each array element is replaced by its own elements, one level deep.

      list.Flatten([[1], [2, 3]]) --> [1, 2, 3] *)

(** list.Zip(a, b)
    The pairs [a[k], b[k]], as many as the shorter list.

      list.Zip([1, 2], [3, 4]) --> [[1, 3], [2, 4]] *)

(** text.Split(sep, s)
    The pieces of a text between the occurrences of a separator, which isn't empty.

      text.Split(",", "a,b") --> ["a", "b"] *)

(** text.Join(sep, xs)
    The texts of the elements, with the separator between.

      text.Join("-", [1, 2]) --> "1-2" *)

(** text.Slice(first, last, s)
    The characters of a text from position first to last, from 1, both included. Outside the text is an error.

      text.Slice(2, 3, "hello") --> "el" *)

(** text.Replace(old, by, s)
    The text with every occurrence of old replaced by another text. Old isn't empty.

      text.Replace("a", "b", "banana") --> "bbnbnb" *)

(** text.Find(part, s)
    The position, from 1, in characters, where part first occurs in a text, or missing.

      text.Find("l", "hello") --> 3 *)

(** text.Contains(part, s)
    Does the text hold the part?

      text.Contains("ell", "hello") --> true *)

(** text.StartsWith(prefix, s)
    Does the text begin with the prefix?

      text.StartsWith("he", "hello") --> true *)

(** text.EndsWith(suffix, s)
    Does the text end with the suffix?

      text.EndsWith("lo", "hello") --> true *)

(** text.Trim(s)
    The text without the spaces and line ends at both ends.

      text.Trim("  a ") --> "a" *)

(** text.Lower(s)
    The text with the letters A to Z in lower case. Other letters are unchanged.

      text.Lower("AbC") --> "abc" *)

(** text.Upper(s)
    The text with the letters a to z in upper case. Other letters are unchanged.

      text.Upper("AbC") --> "ABC" *)

(** text.Repeat(n, s)
    The text n times in a row.

      text.Repeat(3, "ab") --> "ababab" *)

(** text.Lines(s)
    The lines of a text, without their line ends. A last line end opens no empty line.

      text.Lines("a\nb\n") --> ["a", "b"] *)

(** text.PadLeft(n, filler, s)
    The text, filled at the left with the filler up to n characters.

      text.PadLeft(3, "0", "7") --> "007" *)

(** text.PadRight(n, filler, s)
    The text, filled at the right with the filler up to n characters.

      text.PadRight(3, ".", "7") --> "7.." *)

(** text.Chars(s)
    The characters of a text, each as a text.

      text.Chars("héy") --> ["h", "é", "y"] *)

(** text.Char(code)
    The text of one character, from its code point.

      text.Char(65) --> "A" *)

(** text.Code(s)
    The code point of the first character of a text.

      text.Code("A") --> 65 *)

(** text.Number(s)
    The integer or float that a text writes, or missing if it isn't a number.

      text.Number("1.5") --> 1.5 *)

(** text.Decimals(n, x)
    The text of a number with n digits after the point, from 0 to 20, rounded to the nearest, a half away from zero as
    math.Round does; with 0, no point. A float is stored in binary: the one written 2.675 is a little below it, so it gives
    2.67, as in every language that stores floats so.

      text.Decimals(2, 3.14159) --> "3.14"
      text.Decimals(2, 5) --> "5.00"
      text.Decimals(0, 2.5) --> "3"
      text.Decimals(1, -0.25) --> "-0.3"
      text.Decimals(2, 2.675) --> "2.67" *)

(** text.Bytes(s)
    The bytes of a text, as an array of integers from 0 to 255.

      text.Bytes("hi") --> [104, 105] *)

(** text.FromBytes(xs)
    The text made of an array of bytes (integers from 0 to 255).

      text.FromBytes([104, 105]) --> "hi" *)

(** text.Byte(i, s)
    The byte at position i, from 1, of a text.

      text.Byte(1, "A") --> 65 *)

(** text.ByteLength(s)
    The size of a text in bytes.

      text.ByteLength("é") --> 2 *)

(** text.ByteSlice(first, last, s)
    The bytes of a text from position first to last, from 1, both included.

      text.ByteSlice(1, 2, "abc") --> "ab" *)

(** text.ByteFind(part, from, s)
    The byte position, from 1, where part first occurs in a text at or after byte from, or missing.

      text.ByteFind("b", 1, "abc") --> 2 *)

(** text.XorBytes(key, s)
    Each byte of a text xor-ed with the bytes of the key, which repeats. Applying it twice gives the text back.

      text.XorBytes("k", text.XorBytes("k", "hi")) --> "hi" *)

(** math.BitAnd(a, b)
    The bits that are set in both integers.

      math.BitAnd(6, 3) --> 2 *)

(** math.BitOr(a, b)
    The bits that are set in either integer.

      math.BitOr(6, 3) --> 7 *)

(** math.BitXor(a, b)
    The bits that are set in exactly one integer.

      math.BitXor(6, 3) --> 5 *)

(** math.BitNot(a)
    The integer with all its bits flipped.

      math.BitNot(0) --> -1 *)

(** math.BitShift(a, n)
    An integer shifted left by n bits, or right if n is negative.

      math.BitShift(1, 3) --> 8 *)

(** json.Parse(s)
    Reads JSON text: an Ok of the value (objects become dictionaries, null becomes missing), or an Err with the kind "json" and the place of the mistake.

      json.Parse("[1, 2]") --> result.Ok([1, 2]) *)

(** json.Text(x)
    The JSON text of a value, on one line. A function, nan or an infinite float is an error.

      json.Text([1, "a"]) --> "[1,\"a\"]" *)

(** json.Pretty(x)
    The JSON text of a value, with line breaks and two spaces of indent.

      json.Pretty([1]) --> "[\n  1\n]" *)

(** regex.Test(pattern, s)
    Does the pattern match somewhere in the text?

      regex.Test("^a", "abc") --> true *)

(** regex.Find(pattern, s)
    The first match as a struct with Text, Start, Stop (positions in characters, from 1) and Groups, or missing.

      regex.Find("[0-9]+", "ab12c").Text --> "12" *)

(** regex.FindAll(pattern, s)
    Every match, each as in regex.Find, from left to right without overlap.

      value.Len(regex.FindAll("[0-9]", "a1b2")) --> 2 *)

(** regex.Replace(pattern, by, s)
    The text with every match replaced by another text, where $1 to $9 stand for the groups and $$ for a dollar.

      regex.Replace("(a)(b)", "$2$1", "abab") --> "baba" *)

(** regex.Split(pattern, s)
    The pieces of a text between the matches of the pattern.

      regex.Split(",\\s*", "a, b,c") --> ["a", "b", "c"] *)

(** random.Int(low, high)
    A whole number from low to high, both included, each as likely as another.

      random.Int(4, 4) --> 4
      random.Int(1, 6) in 1..6 --> true *)

(** random.Float()
    A float from 0 included to 1 excluded, each as likely as another: random.Float() * 10 is one from 0 to 10.

      random.Float() < 1 --> true *)

(** random.Seed()
    The seed of this run: the one cesia run --seed N gave, or else the one taken from the system at the first draw. The same seed
    gives the same numbers, on every version of Cesia and every system.

      value.IsInt(random.Seed()) --> true *)

(** columns.Kind(xs)
    How an array is stored: "int", "float" or "value".

      columns.Kind([1.5]) --> "float" *)

(** columns.Rows(names, cells, public = false)
    An Ok of a list of maps, one per value.Len(names) cells given row after row; every row of one list of names shares its keys, so a row costs its values only. With public, a SQL name becomes a public Cesia name: created_at gives CreatedAt, id Id; a name that is no identifier, as max(score), stays. Two names that give one key are an Err of kind "names".

      columns.Rows(["id", "name"], [1, "Ana", 2, "Bo"], public = true)?[2].Name --> "Bo" *)

(** columns.Cores()
    The number of processor cores that heavy computations may use. *)

(** columns.Groups(cols)
    Groups the rows that share the same values in a list of key columns: a struct with Ids (the group of each row, from 1), Count and Firsts (the first row of each group).

      columns.Groups([[1, 2, 1]]).Ids --> [1, 2, 1] *)

(** columns.Split(groups, xs)
    The values of a column in each group, in order, without the missing ones.

      columns.Split(columns.Groups([[1, 2, 1]]), [10, 20, 30]) --> [[10, 30], [20]] *)

(** columns.Pick(positions, xs)
    The values at these positions, from 1. Position 0 gives missing.

      columns.Pick([2, 0], [5, 6]) --> [6, missing] *)

(** columns.Join(ys, xs, keep)
    The rows of two key columns that match, as a struct with Left (positions in ys) and Right (positions in xs). If keep is true, a row of ys with no match stays, with Right at 0.

      columns.Join([1, 2], [2, 3], false).Left --> [2] *)

(** columns.Positions(mask)
    The positions, from 1, where a mask of booleans is true.

      columns.Positions([true, false, true]) --> [1, 3] *)

(** columns.Present(xs)
    The elements of an array that are not missing.

      columns.Present([1, missing, 2]) --> [1, 2] *)

(** columns.Csv(s, separator)
    The rows of CSV text (RFC 4180, quotes allowed) as lists of texts. The separator is one character.

      columns.Csv("a,b\n1,2\n", ",") --> [["a", "b"], ["1", "2"]] *)

(** columns.ReadCsv(s, separator)
    CSV text with a header row, as a struct with Names and Columns. An empty cell is missing, and a cell that reads as a number is one.

      columns.ReadCsv("a\n1\n", ",").Columns --> [[1]] *)

(** debug.SetBreak(file, line)
    Sets a breakpoint at a line of a file, which stops the program there. Needs the debug permission. *)

(** debug.ClearBreak(file, line)
    Removes the breakpoint at a line of a file. Needs the debug permission. *)

(** debug.ReadLine()
    A line read from the standard input without its line end, or missing at the end of the input. Needs the debug permission. *)

(** debug.Words(s)
    The words of a command line, split on spaces; a word that is a whole number becomes an integer.

      debug.Words("break 12") --> ["break", 12] *)

(** sys.Read(path)
    The text of a file, in an Ok, or an Err. Needs the read permission. *)

(** sys.Write(path, text)
    Replaces the content of a file with a text, creating it if needed: Ok(missing) or an Err. Needs the write permission. *)

(** sys.Append(path, text)
    Adds a text at the end of a file, creating it if needed: Ok(missing) or an Err. Needs the write permission. *)

(** sys.List(path)
    The sorted names of the entries of a folder, in an Ok, or an Err. Needs the read permission. *)

(** sys.Walk(path)
    The paths of every file under a folder, at any depth, in an Ok, or an Err that names the folder that failed: depth first,
    the names of each folder sorted, each path starting with the folder, as photos/2026/a.jpg, but for ".". A folder is
    entered and not listed; a symbolic link is listed, never followed, so no loop can form. Needs the read permission.
    list.Filter(p -> path.Ext(p) == ".jpg", sys.Walk("photos")?) keeps the photos. *)

(** sys.Remove(path)
    Deletes a file or an empty folder: Ok(missing) or an Err. A symbolic link is deleted, never what it points to. Needs the write permission. *)

(** sys.Stat(path)
    A dictionary with Size (bytes), Modified (seconds since 1970), IsDir, IsFile and IsLink, in an Ok, or an Err. A symbolic link
    counts as what it points to, and IsLink says that it is one. IsFile is true for a regular file only, not for a folder, a device, a FIFO or a socket: their Size says nothing,
    and reading one can use all the memory or hang. Needs the read permission. *)

(** sys.TakeStops(taken)
    Takes the TERM and INT signals from the program, or gives them back, in pairs: for a program that must end by itself
    under --watch, such as a window that saves its state first. With true, TERM and INT no longer end the program, and
    sys.StopAsked tells of them; with false, the program leaves them. Without --watch it does nothing. *)

(** sys.StopAsked()
    Has a TERM or an INT come since the last call? Only after sys.TakeStops(true). *)

(** sys.Rename(from, to)
    Moves a file or a folder: Ok(missing) or an Err. Needs the write permission on both paths. *)

(** sys.Mkdir(path)
    Creates a folder and the missing folders above it, like mkdir -p: Ok(missing) or an Err. Needs the write permission. *)

(** sys.Exists(path)
    Does this file or folder exist? Needs the read permission. *)

(** sys.Env(name)
    The value of an environment variable, or missing. Needs the env permission. *)

(** sys.Sleep(seconds)
    Waits for a number of seconds, letting the other fibers run. *)

(** sys.Run(cmd, args = [], input = "", dir = "", timeout = 0)
    Runs a program to its end, giving it the input text, and gives an Ok of a struct with Code, Out and Err, or an Err if it
    can't start. It runs in the folder dir, or in this program's folder if dir is empty. After timeout seconds, if timeout is
    more than 0, it is killed and its Code is -1, as for a program that a signal ended. Needs the exec permission. *)

(** sys.Start(cmd, args = [])
    Starts a program that keeps running and gives an Ok of its process. Its output and its errors come as one stream. Needs the exec permission. *)

(** sys.ReadOutput(process)
    The next chunk of what a process wrote, in an Ok. An empty text means that its output is closed. *)

(** sys.WriteInput(process, text)
    Sends a text to the input of a process: Ok(missing) or an Err, for instance if the input is closed. *)

(** sys.CloseInput(process)
    Closes the input of a process, so that it sees the end of its input. *)

(** sys.WaitExit(process)
    Waits for a process to end and gives its exit code. The process is finished with after that. *)

(** sys.Stop(process)
    Asks a process to end, with the TERM signal. *)

(** net.Listen(port, host = "127.0.0.1")
    Opens a listening socket: an Ok of the socket, or an Err. Needs the net permission. *)

(** net.Accept(listener)
    Waits for a connection and gives an Ok of its socket. An Err of the kind "stopped" means that the program was asked to stop. *)

(** net.Connect(host, port)
    Opens a connection to a host: an Ok of the socket, or an Err. Needs the net permission. *)

(** net.Read(socket)
    Waits for bytes and gives an Ok of the text received. An empty text means that the other side closed. *)

(** net.Write(socket, text)
    Sends a whole text: Ok(missing) or an Err. *)

(** net.Close(socket)
    Closes a socket, which does nothing if it is already closed. *)

(** net.Arm(socket, seconds)
    Gives a socket a deadline, after which net.Reap closes it. Zero removes the deadline. *)

(** net.Reap()
    Closes the sockets that passed their deadline and gives how many. *)

(** net.OpenCount()
    How many sockets are open. *)

(** net.IsHeader(colon, line)
    Is this a safe header line with its colon at this byte offset? A name with a space or a stray line end is refused, as request smuggling uses them.

      net.IsHeader(4, "host: a") --> true *)

(** c.Open(name, version = missing)
    Loads a shared library by its short name ("m", "sodium"), trying the usual file names of each system, and on macOS the folders of Homebrew: an Ok of the
    library, or an Err. The names with the version come first, then those without it. Needs the ffi permission. *)

(** c.Fn(name, args, ret, lib, callsBack = false)
    Binds a function of a library: an Ok of a Cesia function. The types are given by name ("i32", "f64", "text", "ptr", "void", and so on) or by c.Struct. Set callsBack if the function calls back into Cesia. *)

(** c.Variadic(name, fixed, rest, ret, lib)
    Binds a function with a variable argument list, like printf: the types of the fixed arguments, then those of the ones used. An Ok of a Cesia function. *)

(** c.Callback(f, args, ret)
    Makes a C function pointer from a Cesia function, to pass where C expects a callback. *)

(** c.Struct(fields)
    A C structure type from the types of its fields, laid out as C does. *)

(** c.Size(type)
    The size in bytes of a structure type from c.Struct. *)

(** c.Offset(k, type)
    The offset in bytes of the field k, from 1, of a structure type from c.Struct. *)

(** c.Alloc(size)
    A block of memory of this many bytes, set to zero, outside the Cesia heap. Free it with c.Free. *)

(** c.Free(block)
    Releases a block. It can't be used after that. *)

(** c.Get(type, offset, block)
    The number of this type that a block holds at a byte offset, or that a pointer from C points to, as the arguments
    that qsort gives its comparison: C knows the size of that memory, and nothing checks the offset. *)

(** c.Set(type, offset, value, block)
    Writes a value of this type into a block at a byte offset. *)

(** c.Store(text, offset, block)
    Copies the bytes of a text into a block at a byte offset. *)

(** c.Load(offset, size, block)
    A text of the given number of bytes read from a block at a byte offset. *)

(** c.Text(pointer)
    The text that a pointer from C points to, read up to its zero byte. *)

(** sqlite3.Pool(path, size, wait)
    An Ok of a pool of at most size connections to a SQLite database, created if absent, or to a fresh one in memory with ":memory:", opened when first needed, with WAL for a file and foreign keys. A call that finds them all busy waits at most wait seconds for one. Needs read and write on the path, and no ffi permission. Each connection opens with safe settings: no URI names, defensive, no trusted schema, no extension, nothing attached, texts of 100 MB at most. std/sql is the way to use it. *)

(** sqlite3.Take(pool)
    An Ok of a connection of the pool, waited for if none is free, or an Err with Code "pool" after the wait of the pool, or "closed". sqlite3.Give puts it back. *)

(** sqlite3.Give(conn, pool)
    Puts a connection back in its pool, closed if it is broken or if the pool is. *)

(** sqlite3.Query(text, params, db, limit = missing)
    Runs one statement on a connection, or on one of a pool for this call: an Ok of every row, or of the first limit, as maps whose keys are the column names made public names. A busy database, outside a transaction, is waited for in the fiber. A value SQL doesn't take is an Err with Code "convert". *)

(** sqlite3.One(text, params, db)
    The first row of a statement, as sqlite3.Query reads it: an Ok of a map, or of missing. *)

(** sqlite3.Exec(text, params, db)
    Runs one statement to its end, as sqlite3.Query does: an Ok of {Changed, LastId}. *)

(** sqlite3.Run(text, params, conn)
    Prepares one statement, from a cache of 128 by text, and binds a value to each ?: missing, a Bool, an Int, a Float or a Text. An Ok of a cursor, or an Err; text after the statement that holds another is an Err. *)

(** sqlite3.Next(n, cursor)
    Runs the statement on to n rows: an Ok of {Names, Cells, Done, Changed, LastId}, the cells row after row. NULL is missing, a BLOB a Text of its bytes, a column declared BOOLEAN a Bool. A busy database before the first row, outside a transaction, is waited for in the fiber. *)

(** sqlite3.Script(text, conn)
    Runs several statements, without parameters, on a connection or on one of a pool for this call: an Ok of missing, or the first Err. *)

(** sqlite3.Close(x)
    Closes a cursor before its end, a connection with its statements, or a pool: its free connections now, the others when they come back. Twice is nothing. *)

(** sqlite3.Version()
    An Ok of the version of the SQLite library, or an Err if there is none. *)

(** lapack.Name()
    The name of the LAPACK library found on this machine, or missing if there is none. The functions below then give missing as well, and num falls back on its own code. *)

(** lapack.Solve(a, b)
    The x of a * x = b for a square a, or false if a is singular. Missing without a library. *)

(** lapack.Det(a)
    The determinant of a square matrix. Missing without a library. *)

(** lapack.Qr(a)
    The QR factorization of a matrix with at least as many rows as columns, as a struct with Q and R. Missing without a library. *)

(** lapack.Eigen(a)
    The eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix, as a struct with Values and Vectors. Missing without a library. *)

(** cesia.Version()
    The version of this Cesia, without what follows a + or a -.

      cesia.IsVersion(cesia.Version()) --> true *)

(** cesia.Dev()
    Was this Cesia built from its sources, and not by a release? Then its version text ends in +dev. *)

(** cesia.Binary()
    The path of the running binary of Cesia, from the top of the file system. *)

(** cesia.Fingerprint(text)
    blake2: and the 64 hexadecimal digits of the BLAKE2b-256 of a text, as cesia.lock keeps them.

      cesia.Fingerprint("abc") --> "blake2:bddd813c634239723171ef3fee98579b94964e3bb1cb3e427262c8c068d52319" *)

(** cesia.Compare(a, b)
    Compares two versions number by number, as tags: negative if a comes first, 0 if they are the same, positive after. A leading
    v and what follows a + or a - are left out, and a missing number is 0.

      cesia.Compare("v1.10.0", "1.9") > 0 --> true *)

(** cesia.IsVersion(text)
    Is it numbers between dots, after an optional v?

      cesia.IsVersion("v0.3") --> true *)

(** cesia.Libraries()
    The libraries of this Cesia, as an array of structs with Name and Source: what a project's std/ holds. *)

(** cesia.Toml(text)
    The tables of a cesia.toml or cesia.lock, in an Ok: each a struct with Path (an array of texts), Line and Entries, an entry a
    struct with Key, Value and Line. An Err of {Line, Message} for a text that isn't the TOML Cesia reads.

      cesia.Toml("[a]")?[1].Path --> ["a"] *)

(** cesia.Uses(source)
    The paths of the use lines of a source, as the loader reads them, in an Ok, or an Err of {Line, Message}. Nothing runs.

      cesia.Uses("use std/csv")? --> ["std/csv"] *)

(** cesia.Check(paths, given = {})
    What cesia check finds in files, without running any of them: a list of structs with File, Line, Column, End (the column
    after the span), Severity ("error" or "warning"), Message, Hint (or missing) and Source (the line). A file of a source
    folder of its project is read with its package, a test with it, and any other file alone; given maps a path to its text
    when the disk doesn't hold it. Needs the read permission.

      cesia.Check([], {}) --> [] *)

(** cesia.DocEntry(name)
    What the site of the docs shows of a name or a keyword, or missing: a struct with Name, Module (as DocNames lists it, empty
    for a keyword), Signature, Text (without its examples), Parts (each {Name, Text}: a field, a parameter or the result),
    Examples (the lines that cesia test runs), Builtin (a keyword, or a function written in OCaml), File and Line (where it is
    defined).

      cesia.DocEntry("list.Sort").Builtin --> true
      cesia.DocEntry("match").Module --> ""
      value.IsMissing(cesia.DocEntry("NoSuchName")) --> true *)

(** cesia.Keywords()
    The reserved words of the language, then it, as, _, ? and .., which mean something in some places only: what a highlighter
    colors, and the words the docs explain.

      "defn" in cesia.Keywords() --> true *)

(** cesia.Asset(name)
    A file of the look of the docs site, held in the binary: "site.css" or "site.js". Missing for another name. *)
